# TEXT
d.I("The choice is between Epaphras as Paul's deputy (\"our behalf\") and Epaphras as the church's minister (\"your behalf\").","The choice is between Epaphras as Paul's deputy (\"our behalf\") and Epaphras as the church's minister (\"your behalf\"). **v1.2:** Robertson calls ἡμῶν \"clearly correct\", and the *Lexham Textual Notes* print it. *Uncertain* `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("which explains why the active looks like a correction `[S]`.","which explains why the active looks like a correction `[S]`. **v1.2:** Dunn and Pao both print the active and concede the passive's text-critical claim; Dunn calls the correction \"wholly justified\". Divided `[S: Logos 5]`. For the subject, see Pulpit Notes.")
d.A("  The ESV follows NA28's punctuation for the songs","  **v1.2** `[S: Logos 5]`: \"in all wisdom\" with the teaching is *moderate–high* (Pao; Wright; compare 1:28). The songs as the means of teaching is a slight lean, *moderate* (Pao; Patrick's notes from Beale), with the songs also sung. ἐν [τῇ] χάριτι as \"with thankfulness\" is a slight lean, *moderate* (Pao; the frame of 3:15, 17), and the article has 𝔓⁴⁶ א² B D\\* F G Ψ 6 1505 1739 behind it.")
d.I("and the Claros inscriptions are mid-second century `[S: Logos 3]`.","and the Claros inscriptions are mid-second century `[S: Logos 3]`. **v1.2:** Chrysostom's text has the negative, and he reads \"the worship of the angels\" as an objective genitive. The genitive stays *uncertain*, and the handling is unchanged `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("The shift between \"you\" and \"us\" is part of the passage, and the variant is a scribe smoothing it out `[I]` (Echo row 31).","The shift between \"you\" and \"us\" is part of the passage, and the variant is a scribe smoothing it out `[I]` (Echo row 31). **v1.2:** the KJV and the Vulgate (*dignos nos fecit*) also read \"us\", and the KJV levels 2:13 too (\"you … you\"), so its readers hear no shift at either place `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("The phrase is identical at Eph 5:6.","The phrase is identical at Eph 5:6. **v1.2:** Pao gives omission a slightly stronger case, yet keeps the brackets. Wright keeps the phrase on internal grounds: with it, ἐν οἷς (\"among whom\") and ἐν τούτοις (\"in these things\") at 3:7 can have different antecedents. Divided `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("There is a slight lean to the indicative, *moderate* — Dunn and Pao; Rom 16:18 `[S: Logos 4]`.","There is a slight lean to the indicative, *moderate* — Dunn and Pao; Rom 16:18 `[S: Logos 4]`. **v1.2:** the imperative side is now named: Moule, Lohse, Schweizer, O'Brien, Wright, Harris, Moo and Sumney (Pao's footnote). The lean rests on the clause, not on numbers `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("Divided; *moderate*. NA28's reading equals Eph 6:22 `[S: dig]`.","Divided; *moderate*. NA28's reading equals Eph 6:22 `[S: dig]`. **v1.2:** the *Lexham Textual Notes* add that unaccented ΓΝΩ can be read γνῷ (\"he may know\") or γνῶ (\"I may know\") `[S: Logos 5]`.")
# PULPIT
d.R('- **4:2 "watchful"**','- **4:2 "watchful"**, which lets Gethsemane be heard, as a single echo at most: the wider "Jesus-tradition cluster" was discarded `[S: audit 4]`.')
d.I("The ESV and NASB95 divide on it.","The ESV and NASB95 divide on it. **v1.2:** a slight lean to the thanksgiving, *low–moderate*: Pao for it, while Wright says it is \"impossible to tell\" `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.I("\"Disarmed\" is the same verb as 3:9's \"put off\". \"In him\" could be \"in it\", the cross.","\"Disarmed\" is the same verb as 3:9's \"put off\". \"In him\" could be \"in it\", the cross. **v1.2:** Chrysostom takes Christ as subject, in the true middle (\"having put off from Himself\"), with the triumph at the cross; his English does not settle \"in him\" or \"in it\" `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.R('- **3:16 "with thankfulness"**','- **3:16 "with thankfulness".** Both versions take ἐν χάριτι so, probably rightly (*a slight lean, moderate*: Pao `[S: Logos 5]`). The congregation will not hear that the phrase returns at 4:6, where the ESV has "gracious" (Echo 72).')
d.A('- **2:20 "regulations"**',"""- **2:23 "of no value in stopping the indulgence of the flesh".** The ESV, NASB95 and NIV84 all print this reading. **v1.2:** LSJ gives τιμή no sense "efficacy" and has no τιμή … πρός. ἐν τιμῇ means "in honour" in LSJ and in all five other Greek-Bible uses checked. So the clause leans to "not in any honour" (reading 2, with reading 3 behind it: the KJV, the Vulgate, and Chrysostom for reading 3), *moderate*, and "of no value" falls to *low–moderate*. This goes against Dunn, Pao, Wright and every modern English version, and BDAG, not owned, could restore it, so it is a lexical lean and not a settled result `[S: Logos 5]`. **Preach what every reading shares:** severity to the body does nothing as a remedy for the flesh, *high*. If the clause itself is explained, say that the Greek most naturally reads "not in any honour".""")
d.B('- **2:8, 20 — τὰ στοιχεῖα τοῦ κόσμου.**',"""- **1:22 — who reconciled?** The ESV ("in his body of flesh") and the NASB95 ("in His fleshly body") both have "he" and leave it open. **v1.2:** the subject moves from "God, *moderate–high*" (the dig's first rating) to **open: God or Christ, *moderate* either way.** For Christ: Dunn, Pao and Bird, with Eph 2:16. For God: Wright, and the shape of 1:20. The verb stands only at 1:20, 1:22 and Eph 2:16 (SBLGNT) `[T]`. The move rests on how much weight Ephesians should carry, so it could fairly be contested `[S: Logos 5]`. The preaching point holds either way. In a church using the NIV84, the version has decided for God ("by Christ's physical body").""")
d.A('  - The evidence for a "spirits" sense',"  - Chrysostom reads the sun and moon, because they govern the observance of days: an ancient holder for a calendar sense `[S: Logos 5]`.")
d.R('- **3:16 — the songs.**','- **3:16 — the songs.** NASB95: the songs are the *means* of teaching and admonishing. ESV: they are what is sung. **v1.2:** a slight lean to the NASB95\'s reading, *moderate*, with the songs also sung `[S: Logos 5]`.')
d.I("A congregation with older Bibles will hear these.","A congregation with older Bibles will hear these. **v1.2:** all are now checked against the filed KJV and Clementine Vulgate of the whole letter. The KJV also has \"made us meet\" (1:12), \"you … you\" (2:13), \"seeing that\" (3:9, causal) and \"not in any honour\" (2:23) `[S: Logos 5]`.")
